xnu 0286: the touch tick is the one current after the reports are read

The report thread sampled the display tick at the top of its loop and then spent
up to four report reads (a few milliseconds) draining the part. A vsync that
arrived during the drain left that publish named after the previous tick, and
the next wake -- a report, a few milliseconds later -- found the tick it had
already missed and published a second event for it. Measured in a drag (ring3):
events 6 ms apart followed by a 29 ms hole, 27 of 60 intervals under 10 ms, and
in the same second 18 display ticks with two finished frames and 18 with none
(the framebuffer's rec0/rec2). The UI redraws once per touch event, so the pair
describes the same content twice in one tick and nothing in the next.

Sampling the tick after the drain makes the publish belong to the tick the
position was read in, and one event comes out per tick.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/mondrian_touch.c b/bsd/dev/mondrian_touch.c
--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_touch.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_touch.c
@@ -1690,17 +1690,16 @@
 		sent_y[i] = (int16_t)-1;
 	}
 	for (;;) {
 		int passes;
 		uint32_t tick;
 
 		(void)tsleep((caddr_t)&mondrian_touch_data_addr, PZERO - 1, "mtouch", 1);
 		mondrian_touch_polls++;
-		tick = mondrian_touch_ticks;
 		/*
 		 * Drain what the part has queued. Its line stays low while a report
 		 * waits and one zero byte at the report address releases it, so a
 		 * tick that read a single report threw away the rest of a stream the
 		 * part sends at about 100 Hz. The reports of one tick are coalesced
 		 * into one event a finger below: what the UI wants is the position
 		 * this vsync, not every sample the part made.
 		 */
@@ -1767,16 +1766,29 @@
 		 * per touch event (measured: panel swaps follow the reports answered), and
 		 * the thread wakes about twice a frame, so answering every wake delivers
 		 * 85-120 positions a second at 8 and 17 ms spacings (ring243: `ev gap avg
 		 * 11.7 ms, 27 of them under 10 ms`). The content then advances two steps
 		 * on some frames and one on others, which is a drag that jerks even when
 		 * the frames themselves are even. Reading still happens on every wake;
 		 * what a tick publishes is the newest position, once.
 		 */
+		/*
+		 * Read the tick after the drain, not before it. The drain is up to
+		 * four report reads and takes a few milliseconds; sampled at the top
+		 * of the loop, a vsync that arrives in the middle left this publish
+		 * named after the previous tick, and the next wake (a report, a few
+		 * milliseconds later) then found the tick it had already missed and
+		 * published a second event. Measured: events 6 ms apart followed by a
+		 * 29 ms hole, 27 of 60 intervals under 10 ms in a drag, which commits
+		 * the same content twice in one display tick and not at all in the
+		 * next -- the rec0/rec2 the framebuffer was counting. The tick this
+		 * event belongs to is the one current when the position is known.
+		 */
+		tick = mondrian_touch_ticks;
 		if (tick != emitted_tick) {
 			emitted_tick = tick;
 			for (finger = 0; finger < MONDRIAN_TOUCH_FINGERS; finger++) {
 				if (now[finger]) {
 					mondrian_touch_event_gap();
 					mondrian_touch_events++;
 					mondrian_hid_touch(finger, 1, last_x[finger],
 					    last_y[finger]);
